course: geography
lesson: 29
title: The spice trade and the Indian Ocean
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Today a pepper pot sits on every café table. In medieval Europe a sack of pepper could be worth a fortune, because it grew only in tropical Asia and passed through many hands, each trader adding a profit, before it reached a market in Venice.
explain: Cloves and nutmeg grew only on a few tiny islands in the Moluccas, in today's Indonesia; black pepper came mostly from the Malabar coast of south-west India, around Calicut and Cochin.
explain: Long before Europeans arrived, the Indian Ocean was a busy sea of trade linking East Africa, Arabia, Persia, India, South-East Asia and China, with great ports like Malacca, Calicut and Kilwa and huge Chinese fleets under Zheng He.
explain: The Portuguese did not simply join this trade: they used warships and forts to force others to buy passes and pay duties, and later the Dutch East India Company killed or enslaved most people of the Banda Islands in 1621 to control nutmeg.
explain: In Europa Universalis 5 trade companies and spice goods are tidy mechanics. Real history was rougher and longer: the Indian Ocean was full of trading powers when the Portuguese came, and Europe's merchants won their place largely by force, not by better prices.
fact: Pepper, cinnamon, cloves and nutmeg were very valuable in medieval Europe because they grew only in tropical Asia and passed through many traders.
fact: Venice grew rich buying spices in ports such as Alexandria and Beirut and selling them across Europe, but once ships sailed round Africa straight to India, its spice trade faced strong competition and declined.
fact: Cloves and nutmeg grew only on a few tiny islands in the Moluccas, now part of Indonesia.
fact: Black pepper came mostly from the Malabar coast of south-west India, around Calicut and Cochin, and Vasco da Gama reached Calicut in 1498.
fact: Long before Europeans came, the Indian Ocean was a busy trade world linking East Africa, Arabia, Persia, India, South-East Asia and China.
fact: Malacca on the Malay Peninsula grew into one of Asia's greatest ports in the 1400s by controlling the Strait of Malacca, and the Portuguese captured it in 1511.
fact: Swahili city-states such as Kilwa, Mombasa and Zanzibar traded gold, ivory and other goods from Africa across the Indian Ocean, and rich Kilwa was sacked by the Portuguese in 1505.
fact: Between 1405 and 1433 the Chinese admiral Zheng He led seven great fleets for the Ming across the Indian Ocean, as far as East Africa; his biggest ships were larger than Columbus's, though historians argue about their exact size.
fact: The Portuguese did not simply join the Indian Ocean trade but used warships and forts to force others to buy passes and pay duties.
fact: The English East India Company was founded in 1600 and the Dutch East India Company in 1602 to trade with Asia; merchants shared the cost of costly, risky voyages in them, and both became armed powers, the English one later ruling much of India.
fact: In 1621 the Dutch East India Company killed or enslaved most people of the Banda Islands to control nutmeg.
fact: By the 1700s spices became much cheaper in Europe as more were shipped by sea and plants were grown in new places.
exercise: Why were spices so valuable in medieval Europe? | They grew only in tropical Asia and passed through many traders | They grew only in Europe, in tiny gardens kept by monks, so there were very few | Kings had banned everyone from eating them | They were made in factories in Venice
exercise: Where did cloves and nutmeg grow? | On a few tiny islands in the Moluccas | All over southern Europe, in sunny gardens | In the deserts of Arabia, near the oases | Along the warm coasts of Brazil and Peru
exercise: Which country are the Moluccas part of today? | Indonesia | India | China | The Philippines
exercise: Which coast grew most of the black pepper? | The Malabar coast of south-west India | The rocky west coast of Norway | The Swahili coast of East Africa | The dry coast of Peru and Chile in South America
exercise: Which city was a great pepper port on the Malabar coast? | Calicut | Venice | Lisbon | Samarkand
exercise: When did Vasco da Gama reach Calicut? | In 1498 | In 1337 | In 1600 | In 1066
exercise: Which regions did the Indian Ocean trade link before Europeans came? | East Africa, Arabia, Persia, India, South-East Asia and China | Only Portugal and Spain, which sailed there long before anyone else | Only Britain and North America | Nothing, the ocean was empty of traders
exercise: Why did Malacca become one of Asia's greatest ports? | It controlled the Strait of Malacca | It had the biggest gold mine in Asia | It was the capital of the Mongols | It lay at the mouth of the Nile
exercise: What did Swahili city-states like Kilwa trade across the ocean? | Gold and ivory | Potatoes and maize | Silver from Potosí | Wool and timber
exercise: Who led seven great Ming fleets across the Indian Ocean? | Zheng He | Vasco da Gama | Marco Polo | Kublai Khan
exercise: How far did Zheng He's fleets sail? | As far as East Africa | Only along the coast of China | All the way to America | Around the whole world
exercise: How did the Portuguese make others pay in the Indian Ocean? | Warships and forts forced them to buy passes | They offered cheaper prices than everyone else | They bought up all the spice gardens | They were invited to rule by every port
exercise: When was the English East India Company founded? | In 1600 | In 1498 | In 1405 | In 1807
exercise: What did the Dutch East India Company do in the Banda Islands in 1621? | Killed or enslaved most people to control nutmeg | Built schools and hospitals for the islanders | Bought the islands peacefully from their king | Planted the first pepper on the islands
exercise: Why did spices become cheaper in Europe by the 1700s? | More were shipped by sea and grown in new places | People in Europe suddenly stopped liking spices | Spices began to grow well in Britain's gardens | The price was fixed by law
exercise: Through which ports did Venice buy spices? | Alexandria and Beirut | Calicut and Malacca directly | London and Paris | Lisbon and Seville
test: Why did pepper cost so much more in Europe than in India? | Each trader along the way added a profit and a cost | Indian pepper was mixed with gold dust | European pepper was a different and much rarer plant | It was cheaper in Europe, not dearer
test: My mate says the Indian Ocean was an empty sea till the Portuguese showed up. Right? | No, it was a busy trade world long before them | Yes, nobody sailed it before 1498 | Yes, only the Romans had ever sailed across it before them | No, but only Chinese ships ever used it
test: In the game, a trade company is a handy mechanic. What did the real companies become? | Armed powers, and the English one later ruled much of India | Small shops selling tea and coffee to the people of London | Charities sending food to hungry people in Asia | Clubs for retired sailors
test: How was Portuguese trade different from the trade that was already there? | It used warships and forts to force others to pay | It paid higher prices to every merchant | It used only peaceful bargaining | It was carried out entirely by hired Chinese ships
test: Why were tiny islands like Banda so important? | They were almost the only places nutmeg grew | They held the biggest gold mines in all of Asia | They were the capitals of great empires | They had the best harbours for whaling
test: Why was Malacca a perfect place for a port? | Every ship between India and China passed through its strait | It lay in the middle of a desert | It was far from every trade route, so it was safe from pirates | It had the coldest and cleanest water in the whole of Asia
test: Why did merchants share the cost of voyages in companies? | Voyages were costly and risky, so sharing spread the risk | The king forbade anyone to own a ship on their own | Companies were cheaper to sink | Each merchant only wanted a tiny profit
test: why's pepper so cheap now if it was like gold back then | It is grown and shipped in huge amounts now | Pepper has lost almost all its taste over the years | It is made in factories from sand | Old pepper was a different spice
test: What would have happened to Venice's spice trade after ships sailed around Africa straight to India? | It faced strong new competition and declined | It became the only spice route to Europe forever | The whole city of Venice moved to India to trade | Nothing, as Venice never traded spices at all
test: Zheng He's biggest ships were larger than Columbus's. Do historians agree how large? | No, they argue about the exact size | Yes, they were exactly 1 kilometre long | Yes, they were smaller than rowing boats | No, Zheng He never had any ships at all
test: Kilwa was very rich in the 1300s. What happened to it in 1505? | It was sacked by the Portuguese | It became the capital of China | It sank into the sea | It was bought by the Dutch for gold
exam: Identify the principal reason for which spices commanded exceptionally high prices in medieval Europe. | They were cultivated solely in tropical Asia and passed through numerous intermediaries | They were cultivated throughout Europe in negligible quantities by monastic houses alone | Their consumption was restricted by law to the clergy | They were manufactured exclusively in the workshops of Venice
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Indian Ocean prior to the arrival of the Portuguese? | An extensive commercial world linking East Africa, Arabia, India, South-East Asia and China | An expanse of sea traversed by no merchant vessels whatsoever prior to the year 1498 | A sea navigated exclusively by vessels of Roman origin | A region closed to commerce by the decree of the Ming emperor
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the conduct of the Portuguese in the Indian Ocean. | They employed warships and fortresses to compel others to purchase passes and pay duties | They participated in the existing trade solely by peaceful competition | They declined to establish any fortified positions in Asia | They were invited by every port to assume its government
exam: It is given that Admiral Zheng He commanded seven expeditions between 1405 and 1433. Ascertain the furthest region attained by these fleets. | East Africa | The Americas | Western Europe | Antarctica's coast
